Q: How to import MLX90640 thermal camera image data into OpenCV via i2c on Raspberry Pi in C++? A: <p>you already got there in Update 5, just dropping the condensed version as an answer so it isn't buried in the question.</p>
<p>you don't need the framebuffer / v4l2loopback / gstreamer route at all. MLX90640_CalculateTo already hands back 768 floats, so point a cv::Mat straight at that buffer and let opencv do the colour map:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>// mlx90640To is float[768], 32x24 (w x h)
Mat temp(24, 32, CV_32FC1, mlx90640To);   // rows=24, cols=32

Mat norm;
normalize(temp, norm, 0, 255, NORM_MINMAX);
norm.convertTo(norm, CV_8U);

Mat color;
applyColorMap(norm, color, COLORMAP_JET);
resize(color, color, Size(320, 240), 0, 0, INTER_CUBIC);

imshow(&quot;thermal&quot;, color);
waitKey(1);
</code></pre>
<p>if you want the sensor side (i2c bring-up, CalculateTo, refresh rate vs frame ready), that part is here: <a href="https://0xeax.com/hardware/pico-usb-thermal-camera/mlx90640-i2c-bringup" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://0xeax.com/hardware/pico-usb-thermal-camera/mlx90640-i2c-bringup</a> (a Pico not a Pi, but the Melexis driver and math are the same).</p>

Q: Map&lt;?, Optional&lt;T&gt;&gt; to Map&lt;?, T&gt; A: <p>Here is a generic function which achieves the stated goal using <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/util/Optional.html#stream()" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Optional.stream</code></a> and <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/util/stream/Stream.html#flatMap(java.util.function.Function)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Stream.flatMap</code></a> to unwrap the non-empty <code>Optional</code> values and skip the empty ones:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>private static &lt;K, T&gt; Map&lt;K, T&gt; convert(Map&lt;K, Optional&lt;T&gt;&gt; input) {
    return input.entrySet().stream()
            .flatMap(e -&gt; e.getValue().stream().map(v -&gt; Map.entry(e.getKey(), v)))
            .collect(Collectors.toMap(Map.Entry::getKey, Map.Entry::getValue));
}
</code></pre>
<p>This converts each <code>Optional</code> to a <code>Stream</code> of zero or one <code>Entry&lt;K, Optional&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code> values using <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/util/Optional.html#stream()" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Optional.stream</code></a>.  These mapped entries — none of which contain an empty <code>Optional</code> — are each mapped to an <code>Entry&lt;K, T&gt;</code> containing the unwrapped <code>Optional</code>.  These <code>Optional</code>-sourced streams are flat mapped into a single stream of elements using <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/util/stream/Stream.html#flatMap(java.util.function.Function)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Stream.flatMap</code></a>. Finally, the <code>Entry</code> elements are converted back to a <code>Map</code> using <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/util/stream/Collectors.html#toMap(java.util.function.Function,java.util.function.Function)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Collectors.toMap</code></a>.</p>

Q: Converting xml to json in Golang A: <pre><code>// Source - https://stackoverflow.com/a/71869525
// Posted by Santosh Anand
// Retrieved 2026-07-29, License - CC BY-SA 4.0

package main

import (
    &quot;encoding/json&quot;
    &quot;fmt&quot;
    &quot;strings&quot;

    xj &quot;github.com/basgys/goxml2json&quot;
)

// ToObject - convert string to any given struct
func ToObject(value string, object interface{}) error {
    err := json.Unmarshal([]byte(value), &amp;object)
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }
    return nil
}
func main() {
    xml := strings.NewReader(`&lt;?xml version=&quot;1.0&quot; encoding=&quot;UTF-8&quot;?&gt;
    &lt;osm version=&quot;0.6&quot; generator=&quot;CGImap 0.0.2&quot;&gt;
     &lt;bounds minlat=&quot;54.0889580&quot; minlon=&quot;12.2487570&quot; maxlat=&quot;54.0913900&quot; maxlon=&quot;12.2524800&quot;/&gt;
     &lt;foo&gt;bar&lt;/foo&gt;
    &lt;/osm&gt;`)

    converter, err := xj.Convert(xml)
    if err != nil {
        panic(&quot;ERROR converting xml to json&quot;)
    }
    osmStruct := &amp;ExampleStruct{}
    ToObject(converter.String(), &amp;osmStruct)

    b, _ := json.Marshal(osmStruct)
    fmt.Println(string(b))
}

// ExampleStruct -
type ExampleStruct struct {
    Osm Osm `json:&quot;osm&quot;`
}

// Osm -
type Osm struct {
    Version   string `json:&quot;,omitempty&quot;`
    Generator string `json:&quot;,omitempty&quot;`
    Bounds    Bounds `json:&quot;bounds&quot;`
    Foo       string `json:&quot;foo&quot;`
}

// Bounds -
type Bounds struct {
    Minlat string `json:&quot;,omitempty&quot;`
    Minlon string `json:&quot;,omitempty&quot;`
    Maxlat string `json:&quot;,omitempty&quot;`
    Maxlon string `json:&quot;,omitempty&quot;`
}
</code></pre>
